Fixed. `pip` is working again.

**What was wrong:** your `pip` package files had been deleted from disk, but its metadata was left behind. `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info/` was fully intact while the `pip/` module directory next to it was gone — 841 of 850 recorded files missing. So `/usr/local/bin/pip3` still existed and ran, and died on line 5 with `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'`. That error is why installs failed: pip never got far enough to look at a package index.

This also explains why `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade` did *not* fix it. `ensurepip` checks whether pip is installed by looking for the `dist-info` metadata, found the orphaned directory, concluded `Requirement already satisfied`, and did nothing. The usual repair is a silent no-op on this specific breakage.

**What I did:** extracted Python's own bundled wheel, `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl`, straight into `site-packages`. A wheel is a zip, so this needed no working pip and no network.

**Verification:** all 850 recorded files now present (0 missing); `pip --version`, `python3 -m pip`, and `pip list` all work; `pip check` reports no broken requirements; and a real `pip install` of a wheel completed successfully.

Two things worth flagging:

- **I could not test an actual PyPI download** — this session has network disabled by policy, so I verified only the offline path. If installs still fail for you, the remaining error will be a network/proxy one, which is a different problem from the one I fixed.
- **The repo itself is untouched.** Every change was to `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages`; `/app` still contains only the `.gnomon/` harness surface. Nothing to commit.

The likely cause of the original deletion is an interrupted uninstall or an aggressive cleanup of `site-packages`. If you want to guard against a repeat, installing into a virtualenv instead of the system interpreter avoids it — but note that setuptools and wheel are also absent here, which will matter if you build packages from source.

